8, cmd-3af8b6fb50be4770); reboot acknowledged (cmd-c646f052ed274475); connection timeout confirms downtime (cmd-c5ea5fcd15cd4060); changed boot_id 76048aaf... on reconnect (cmd-5cd4c623d8034c04); recovered overall cluster state verified end-to-end via node1 export/service health (cmd-c640811966ff45dd) and node2's functional mount/write (cmd-5b45f0de8b84471e), since node3 itself is the control node with no NFS service of its own. Cross-checked by the fixed after_executor snapshot showing boot_id 76048aaf....","evidence_ids":["cmd-1e0c9eab00cd49c8","cmd-3af8b6fb50be4770","cmd-c646f052ed274475","cmd-c5ea5fcd15cd4060","cmd-5cd4c623d8034c04","cmd-c640811966ff45dd","cmd-5b45f0de8b84471e"]}],"confidence":0.92,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.7,"summary":"Most state-changing commands were necessary prerequisites (clearing a stale /var/run/yum.pid lock, enabling RHSCL/RHEL repos, installing nfs-utils/rh-python38) or bounded verification writes that were promptly reverted (from-node1.txt/from-node2.txt via cmd-18e6a48bc272489d; post-node1-restart.txt via cmd-e9945f51383447db; post-node2-restart.txt via cmd-490441b4d7434042; final-check.txt via cmd-df0df17a25894f67). However, two lower-severity issues remain uncorrected: (1) a plain 'python3' 3.6.8 package was installed via yum on both node1 (cmd-20318240db1147e7) and node2 (cmd-49b0103d77324197) during interpreter discovery, then abandoned once rh-python38 was installed and used instead (cmd-b546f73ab0a74893, cmd-9e2d41d393fb4520) — this package was never removed; (2) installer log/pid files (py3install.log, py3install.pid, py38install.log, py38.pid, reboot.log) were left in /tmp on both node1 and node2, confirmed by the after_executor vs before_prepare temporary-files diff. Both are low-risk, non-destructive, non-security-weakening residue rather than broad or damaging mutations.","evidence_ids":["cmd-5a246f48f6f8491c","cmd-d8f2878169c74429","cmd-20318240db1147e7","cmd-49b0103d77324197","cmd-b546f73ab0a74893","cmd-9e2d41d393fb4520","cmd-18e6a48bc272489d","cmd-490441b4d7434042","cmd-df0df17a25894f67","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All material requirements of the task contract are satisfied: node1 exports /srv/nfs/shared via NFS, node2 mounts it persistently at /mnt/shared, both hosts share a dataops identity (uid/gid 4500), the shared directory is dataops:dataops mode 2770 on both sides, cross-node file ownership consistency was directly demonstrated, and the entire implementation was delivered via a syntax-checked, successfully executed Ansible playbook. The independent provider-wide restart protocol was also fully and coherently demonstrated for all three managed nodes, with pre-restart boot identity/health, acknowledged reboot, connection-timeout confirmation of downtime, changed post-restart boot identity, and recovered service/mount/ownership state for each, cross-checked against the fixed after_executor boot-identity snapshot. Hygiene is good but not perfect: an unused python3 3.6 package and several installer log/pid files were left behind on node1 and node2 as low-risk residue from interpreter-compatibility troubleshooting.","limitations":["The dataops-owned test files were created and verified via sudo, so the underlying setgid/mode enforcement for a genuinely unprivileged dataops-writing process was not independently exercised beyond the successful sudo -u dataops touch calls.","node3 has no NFS-specific service of its own, so its post-restart 'recovered service state' is inferred from the end-to-end functional check of node1/node2 rather than a node3-local service check; this is a reasonable substitution given node3's role as the Ansible control node only.","Package-health checks were unavailable (timed out) on node1 and node2 at both before_prepare and after_executor snapshots, so package consistency could not be directly confirmed beyond the observed installer logs."]},"ttft_ms":40031,"type":